Q: Why is `std::pair&lt;int, int&gt;` not designed to be trivially copyable? A: <p>The checks for <code>std::is_trivially_copyable</code> fail because <code>std::pair</code> does not have a trivial copy-assignment operator. The defined operator does nothing more than <code>this-&gt;first = other.first; this-&gt;second = other.second;</code> therefore at first glance one may assume that the default operator would work.</p>
<p>However, <code>std::pair</code> also supports references and you cannot use the default assignment operator for structs with reference members. For pairs of references the assignment shall assign to the referenced values. The following is desired behavior:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>
std::pair&lt;int, int&gt; compute(int a, int b)
{
    return std::make_pair(a + b, a - b);
}

int main()
{
    int a = 0, b = 0;
    std::pair&lt;int&amp;, int&amp;&gt;{a, b} = compute(3, 4);
    assert(a == 7);
    assert(b == -1);
}
</code></pre>
<p>If this code looks weird, remember that this is the exact use case for which <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/utility/tuple/tie" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::tie</code> uses <code>std::tuple</code></a>. In addition, this was valid code in C++03, therefore there were also concerns about breaking existing codes with little benefit if this behavior had been changed with C++11 (which introduced the traits and default operators).</p>
<p>This reasoning is also expressed in an <a href="https://lists.llvm.org/pipermail/cfe-dev/2020-August/066625.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">LLVM mailing list thread</a> I was able to find.</p>
<p>As for your original use case of speeding up <code>std::vector</code> resizing: <code>std::vector</code> does not require copy-assignment, only copy construction and destruction. Therefore they may use <code>std::is_trivially_copy_constructible_v&lt;…&gt;</code> for the <code>memcpy</code> and then a second <code>std::is_trivially_destructible_v&lt;…&gt;</code> to skip the destructor loop (though the compiler should be able to delete that one on its own). Both are true for <code>std::pair&lt;int, int&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>However, the copy assignment is still required for other operations like inserting or deleting in the middle of a vector. Therefore yes, it would be nice if <code>pair</code> supported that trait. From what I can tell, compilers do not generate particularly efficient code for <code>std::copy</code> with arrays of pairs, especially on <code>-O2</code> or equivalent.</p>

Q: SVG Positioning A: <p>There is a shorter alternative to the previous answer. SVG Elements can also be grouped by nesting svg elements:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
  xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt;
  &lt;svg x="10"&gt;
    &lt;rect x="10" y="10" height="100" width="100"
          style="stroke:#ff0000;fill: #0000ff"/&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;
  &lt;svg x="200"&gt;
    &lt;rect x="10" y="10" height="100" width="100"
          style="stroke:#009900;fill: #00cc00"/&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>The two rectangles are identical (apart from the colors), but the parent svg elements have different x values.</p>
<p><a href="http://tutorials.jenkov.com/svg/svg-element.html" rel="noreferrer">See http://tutorials.jenkov.com/svg/svg-element.html</a>.</p>

Q: How to show how many assertions were performed during an execution of tests in maven A: <p>I had a similar concern that due to try...catch blocks omitting sections of code or other faulty logic, that tests could appear to pass and give false confidence. So I created this wrapper class:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class AssertionTracker {
    public static void init() {
        nAssertions = 0;
    }

    public static void assertw(boolean criterion) {
        assert(criterion);
        nAssertions++;
    }

    public static int getNumAssertions() {
        System.out.println(&quot;nAssertions = &quot; + nAssertions);
        return nAssertions;
    }

    private static int nAssertions = 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>instead of assert I use <code>AssertionTracker.</code><em><code>assertw</code></em> and each test ends with:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>assert(AssertionTracker.getNumAssertions() == &lt;expression&gt;);
</code></pre>
<p>I use JUnit5 and Gradle. I know that Gradle does have <code>maxParallelForks</code> which would break this simple scheme, but I can't use it on my project, and the scheme could be extended by storing <code>nAssertions</code> separately for each stream, in a map indexed by test name for example.</p>
